                <pb facs="0212" n="200" sameAs="frus:frus1945v04/pg200"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">874.00/4–2745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The United States Representative in Bulgaria</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Barnes</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sofia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-27T14:00:00+02:00">April 27, 1945—2
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 3:40 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>232. The Communist campaign against G. M. Dimitrov and all Agrarian elements that
                    support him is continuing. Dimitrov has now been placed under house arrest and
                    political orators and the press denounce him almost daily, alleging Fascist
                    tendencies. These articles and speeches sometimes contain only slightly veiled
                    reference to one of the basic reasons for Communist determination to eliminate
                    him, namely his pro-British (one might even say pro-Anglo-Saxon) tendencies.
                    Petkov, who a few months ago replaced him as secretary of the Agrarian party,
                    was asked recently by the Communist Regent Pavlov to give in to Communist Party
                    pressure and publicly attack Dimitrov. Petkov replied that, if necessary in the
                    interest of preserving the FF, he would comply but that he could base his attack
                    only on Dimitrov’s faith in Anglo-Saxon democracy. Pavlov of course would not
                    consent to this.</p>
                <p>Repeated to AmPolAd as 115 and to Moscow as 105.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Barnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
